WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.520
So the history
of MDMA begins in 1912,

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:08.420
when it was first synthesized
and patented by Merck,

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:11.890
but they didn't do
anything at all until 1927.

00:00:11.890 --> 00:00:14.730
And Merck did some preclinical
studies in animals,

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:16.520
and found nothing of interest

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.610
and they decided to just
shelve the compound.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:21.700
This is around the time
their patent is gonna expire.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:25.020
The next we know of MDMA is

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:27.570
the army chemical warfare
service, MK-Ultra,

00:00:27.570 --> 00:00:29.330
looking for mind control drugs,

00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:33.400
and then Sasha Shulgin, who
was an incredible chemist,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.550
he started looking for new
drugs for psychotherapy

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.310
and for personal growth and spirituality.

00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:44.560
And he re-synthesized MDMA,
experimented with it on himself,

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:48.830
and felt that it had incredible
potential for therapy.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:52.670
And so he gave it to Leo
Zeff, who was the leader

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:55.730
of the Underground Psychedelic
Psychotherapy community.

00:00:55.730 --> 00:00:59.610
From the middle '70s to the early '80s,

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:02.160
roughly half a million
doses of MDMA were used

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:04.210
in therapeutic growth settings,

00:01:04.210 --> 00:01:06.860
mostly by people that Leo had trained,

00:01:06.860 --> 00:01:08.580
and it was found to be useful

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.640
in a wide variety of conditions.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:14.040
But it leaked out of that
community, became sold as Ecstasy,

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.970
and this was at the time of
Nancy Reagan and "Just Say No",

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.900
and the rise of the drug war again,

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.700
and so it was clear that it was doomed.

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.970
So this was an incredible
opportunity to gather support

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:26.410
to prepare for the defense of MDMA

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.940
once the Drug Enforcement
Administration finally decided

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:30.993
to move against it.

00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:34.860
It was in Dallas at the Starck Club

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:37.440
where Ecstasy really became popular,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:41.970
MDMA became popular as Ecstasy
in a dance club setting.

00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:44.920
George Bush, all sorts of
people participated here,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.220
and this is what attracted
the attention of the DEA.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:49.020
So in the summer of '84,

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.950
DEA moved to put MDMA in Schedule I.

00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:53.730
They only knew about it as a party drug,

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.250
they didn't know about
it as a therapy drug.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.510
So in '86, I started MAPS as a non-profit.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.610
MAPS is the Multidisciplinary Association

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.600
for Psychedelic Studies.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:06.990
MDMA is the most gentle of
the all the psychedelics,

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.320
that's why I felt it
would be the first one

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:10.822
to make it through the system.

00:02:10.822 --> 00:02:13.405
 

